Given the function $f(x)=a\ln x-x(a\ne 0)$;

    $(1)$ If $a=1$, find the intervals of monotonicity for the function $f(x)$;

$(2)$ When $a > 0$, find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $(0,2]$.

s:
Solution:

$(1)$ The derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'(x)= \frac{a}{x}-1= \frac{-x+a}{x},x > 0$. When $a=1$, $f'(x)= \frac{-x+1}{x},x > 0$,

$f'(x) > 0 \Rightarrow 0 < x < 1, f'(x) < 0 \Rightarrow x > 1$,  

Therefore, the interval of increase for $f(x)$ is $(0,1)$, and the interval of decrease is $(1,+\infty)$;

$(2)$ When $a > 0$, from $(1)$ $f'(x) > 0 \Rightarrow 0 < x < a, f'(x) < 0 \Rightarrow x > a$,

   On the interval $(0,2]$

${1}^{\circ}$ When $0 < a < 2$, $f'(x) > 0 \Rightarrow 0 < x < a, f'(x) < 0 \Rightarrow a < x \leq 2$, therefore $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(0,a)$ and decreasing on the interval $(a,2]$, $\therefore f(x)_{\max}=f(a)=a\ln a-a$;

${2}^{\circ}$ When $a \geq 2$, $f'(x) > 0 \Rightarrow 0 < x \leq 2$, therefore $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(0,2]$, $\therefore f(x)_{\max}=f(2)=2\ln 2-2$;

In summary, when $0 < a < 2$, $f(x)_{\max}=a\ln a-a$, when $a \geq 2$, $f(x)_{\max}=2\ln 2-2$. Therefore, the final answer is $\boxed{f(x)_{\max}=\begin{cases} a\ln a-a & \text{if } 0 < a < 2 \\ 2\ln 2-2 & \text{if } a \geq 2 \end{cases}}$.

