(2) Find the solution set when $a$ is a constant.

s:
(1) According to the problem, when $a = 2$, the inequality becomes $x^2 + 2x - 3 \leq 0$.
The solution can be obtained as: $-3 \leq x \leq 1$.
Hence, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{[-3, 1]}$.

(2) According to the problem, when $a$ is a constant, the equation $x^2 + 2x + 1 - a^2 = 0$ has two roots, which are $x_1 = -1 + a$ and $x_2 = -1 - a$.
When $a > 0$, $x_1 > x_2$, and the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{[-1 - a, -1 + a]}$;
When $a = 0$, $x_1 = x_2$, and the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{-1\}}$;
When $a < 0$, $x_1 < x_2$, and the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{[-1 + a, -1 - a]}$.

In summary:
When $a > 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{[-1 - a, -1 + a]}$;
When $a = 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{-1\}}$;
When $a < 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{[-1 + a, -1 - a]}$.

